Hi all!

Some additional notes on this patch set:

This has been tested together with the underlying v5 version of the 
RISC-V Dovetail patches in QEMU, as well as on the StarFive VisionFive 2 
board.

These tests were done in my home lab, but I plan to integrate one of my 
boards into the Xenomai-images lab next week.

All tests of the evl testsuite pass besides the ptrace-sync test. See 
the mail "evl: ptrace continue without hardware singlestepping support" 
from 19th of February on this open issue.

Please find the results of the latency tests attached.

* All tests were done with two isolated cores.
* The tests show one latmus thread per core (isolated and system cores), 
started with "latmus -m --user -p 200 -P 99 -c 0".
* In the stressed version, stressing was done with "stress-ng --procfs 1 
--null 1 --getdent 1 --sock 1 --cpu 1 --vm 1 --vm-bytes 64M --timer 1 
--hrtimers 1 --switch 1 --fp 1".

While testing different background loads, I noticed that stressing 
virtual memory and the MMC at the same time leads to high latency 
spikes. The memlat-correlation.png shows a correlation between 
concurrent memory access latency and latmus latency measurements while 
stressing the system in this way. The results indicate that the 
latencies occur when the measurement thread is evicted from the cache 
and competes with the MMC for the DDR controller. While I can only 
observe this with targeted stressing, this hardware limitation should be 
kept in mind.

Thanks to Philippe, Jan and Florian for answering all my questions and 
the guidance at this point. :)

Best,
Tobias

